```markdown

Three independent fixes: detached worker lifecycle, provider selection (inline key + flat-rate bucket), and chunk index time-ordering.

### 1. Detached worker launch (survives scheduler tick session end)

The session-scoped background process was SIGTERM'd when the scheduler tick session ended. Fix: launch through a `/tmp` launcher script using `setsid nohup`, write a pid file, and poll via `kill -0` + log tail — never via the child of a session-bound fork.

```cpp
// scheduler.cpp — detached worker dispatch
#include <sys/types.h>
#include <sys/stat.h>
#include <unistd.h>
#include <signal.h>
#include <cstdio>
#include <fstream>
#include <string>

struct WorkerHandle { std::string pid_file; std::string log_file; };

// 1) Materialize a launcher script in /tmp. The shell performs setsid+nohup,
//    so the worker is reparented to init and leaves the scheduler's session.
std::string write_launcher(const std::string& bin, const std::string& args,
                           const std::string& log_file, const std::string& pid_file) {
    std::string path = "/tmp/worker_launch_" + std::to_string(getpid()) + ".sh";
    std::ofstream out(path);
    out << "#!/bin/sh\n"
        << "exec setsid nohup '" << bin << "' " << args
        << " >'" << log_file << "' 2>&1 &\n"
        << "echo $! >'" << pid_file << "'\n";
    out.close();
    chmod(path.c_str(), 0755);
    return path;
}

// 2) Launch and forget. No kill on the tick session teardown can reach the
//    worker: its session id now differs from the scheduler's.
bool launch_detached(const std::string& launcher) {
    return system((launcher + " >/dev/null 2>&1").c_str()) == 0;
}

// 3) Poll: kill -0 for liveness, tail for output. Distinguish "died with
//    zero output" (crash) from "finished silently" (exit code 0, log nonempty).
enum class WorkerState { RUNNING, EXITED_OK, CRASHED };
WorkerState poll_worker(const WorkerHandle& h, std::string* tail) {
    pid_t pid = 0;
    std::ifstream pf(h.pid_file); pf >> pid;
    if (pid <= 0 || kill(pid, 0) != 0) {               // ESRCH or EPERM
        std::string log; { std::ifstream lf(h.log_file); lf >> log; }
        *tail = log;
        return log.empty() ? WorkerState::CRASHED : WorkerState::EXITED_OK;
    }
    char buf[4096]; size_t n = 0;
    FILE* f = popen(("tail -n 20 '" + h.log_file + "'").c_str(), "r");
    if (f) { n = fread(buf, 1, sizeof(buf) - 1, f); pclose(f); }
    buf[n] = '\0'; *tail = buf;
    return WorkerState::RUNNING;
}
```

The scheduler tick keeps a `WorkerHandle` map; each tick calls `poll_worker` and only relaunches when the worker is `CRASHED` or `EXITED_OK` with a non-empty completion marker in the log.

### 2. Provider selection: inline `api_key`, flat-rate worker bucket

Two fleet rules were violated: the worker ran on `deepseek-foreman` (foreman's own PAYG bucket) and on a provider block whose key resolves only via `api_key_env` — hermes resolves `Bearer None` at call time when the env var is absent in the detached session, yielding HTTP 401.

```cpp
// provider.cpp — selection gate
struct Provider {
    std::string name;
    std::string api_key;       // INLINE key, non-empty
    std::string api_key_env;   // env indirection — unsafe for detached workers
    bool flat_rate_prepaid;    // fleet rule: never foreman's PAYG bucket
    std::string model;
};

bool usable_for_worker_dispatch(const Provider& p) {
    if (!p.flat_rate_prepaid)  return false;      // rule 1: prepaid bucket only
    if (p.api_key.empty())     return false;      // rule 2: INLINE key required
    return true;                                  // ollama-cloud / minimax-m3
}
```

Minimax provider block fix — add the inline key so resolution never falls back to `Bearer None`:

```yaml
# providers.yaml
providers:
  minimax:
    base_url: https://api.minimax.chat/v1
    api_key: sk-minimax-inline-****      # INLINE — verified HTTP 200
    api_key_env: MINIMAX_API_KEY         # kept for compat, never sole source
    models: [minimax-m3]
  ollama-cloud:
    base_url: http://ollama-cloud:11434/v1
    api_key: sk-ollama-inline-****       # INLINE
    models: [minimax-m3]
    flat_rate_prepaid: true              # worker bucket
```

Dispatch now iterates `usable_for_worker_dispatch` providers in priority order and prefers `ollama-cloud` (prepaid, inline key, model pinned to `minimax-m3`).

### 3. Chunk index time-ordering under split batched inserts

Split batched inserts (60 rows → 54 chunks) dispatched per-row shuffled the chunk array, so `expired_chunks` prefix-scan assumed `[oldest..newest]` and `downsample_candidates` assumed newest-is-last — both broke. Fix: assign a monotonic `seq` before splitting, then sort by (bucket, end-time, seq) after insert.

```cpp
// chunk_store.cpp — restore ordering invariants
struct Chunk {
    int64_t seq;        // monotonic insert sequence, assigned pre-split
    int64_t bucket;
    int64_t start_ns;   // min row timestamp
    int64_t end_ns;     // max row timestamp
};

// Called once per batched insert, after the split fan-out completes.
void restore_chunk_order(std::vector<Chunk>& chunks) {
    std::sort(chunks.begin(), chunks.end(),
              [](const Chunk& a, const Chunk& b) {
                  if (a.bucket != b.bucket) return a.bucket < b.bucket;
                  if (a.end_ns  != b.end_ns)  return a.end_ns  < b.end_ns;  // time-ordered
                  return a.seq   < b.seq;                                  // stable tiebreak
              });
    // Rebuild the prefix index in sorted order so:
    //   expired_chunks:      scan prefix while chunk.end_ns < cutoff
    //   downsample_candidates: newest chunk is chunks.back()
}
```

The single insert path now does: assign `seq` to all 60 rows → split into 54 chunks (chunks inherit min/max row ts) → `restore_chunk_order` → commit index. Any code that appends chunks individually (e.g. late-arriving rows) calls `restore_chunk_order` on the affected bucket too.

**Root cause.** `handleChat` translated the NL message into a fully-populated `types.SearchRequest`, then collapsed it to `SearchFlows(sr.Query, sr.Limit)` — silently dropping `TimeRange`, `Categories`, and `Outcomes`. A time-only question ("flows from the last hour") produced an empty query with a real window, and the window never reached the store. The fix was **wiring, not reimplementation**: grep showed all the primitives already existed — `types.SearchRequest` carried the filters, `store.QueryFlows` already filtered by phase/outcome/`start_time` window (mirrored in `handleSearch`), and `parseTranslateResponse` already parsed phases/outcomes. Three small changes:

**1. Route chat through `QueryFlows`, threading every filter** (`chat/handler.go`):

```go
// Before (bug): dropped TimeRange/Categories/Outcomes
sr, _ := h.trans.Translate(ctx, message)
return h.store.SearchFlows(sr.Query, sr.Limit)   // ← filters lost

// After (fix): the one-line wiring change
func (h *Handler) HandleChat(ctx context.Context, message string) ([]store.Flow, error) {
	sr, err := h.trans.Translate(ctx, message)
	if err != nil {
		return nil, err
	}
	return h.store.QueryFlows(sr.Query, sr.Limit, sr.TimeRange, sr.Categories, sr.Outcomes), nil
}
```

`SearchFlows` stays as a thin legacy wrapper (`return s.QueryFlows(query, limit, nil, nil, nil)`) for old call sites; chat no longer uses it. The pre-existing `handleSearch` path was already correct and is now the shared route.

**2. Extend `translateSystemPrompt` to emit `time_range` + inject current UTC time** (`chat/translate.go`) — relative windows ("last hour") need a concrete anchor, otherwise the model guesses "now" and the window is wrong:

```go
func translateSystemPrompt(now time.Time) string {
	return fmt.Sprintf(`You translate a chat message into a structured search request.
Current time: %s (UTC).

Respond with JSON only:
{"query": string, "categories": [string], "outcomes": [string],
 "time_range": {"start": string|null, "end": string|null}}

Rules:
- query: free-text terms, or "" when the message only expresses filters.
- categories: flow phases to include; [] when unspecified.
- outcomes: flow outcomes to include; [] when unspecified.
- time_range: RFC3339 UTC timestamps; interpret relative windows
  ("last hour", "past 30 days") against the current time above.
  Use null for an open bound ("before 2024-01-01" ->
  {"start": null, "end": "2024-01-01T00:00:00Z"}). Use null/null when no
  time constraint is expressed.`, now.UTC().Format(time.RFC3339))
}
```

**3. `parseTranslateResponse` parses the nullable RFC3339 window; `query==""` is rejected only when no window is present:**

```go
type timeRangeWire struct {
	Start *time.Time `json:"start"`
	End   *time.Time `json:"end"`
}

type translateWire struct {
	Query      string         `json:"query"`
	Categories []string       `json:"categories"`
	Outcomes   []string       `json:"outcomes"`
	TimeRange  *timeRangeWire `json:"time_range"`
}

func parseTranslateResponse(raw string) (types.SearchRequest, error) {
	var w translateWire
	if err := json.Unmarshal([]byte(raw), &w); err != nil {
		return types.SearchRequest{}, fmt.Errorf("parse translate response: %w", err)
	}
	req := types.SearchRequest{Query: strings.TrimSpace(w.Query), Categories: w.Categories, Outcomes: w.Outcomes}
	if w.TimeRange != nil {
		tr := &types.TimeRange{Start: w.TimeRange.Start, End: w.TimeRange.End}
		if tr.Start != nil || tr.End != nil { // normalize null/null → no window
			req.TimeRange = tr
		}
	}
	// A pure time-range question legitimately has query=="" — only reject
	// when there is no window either.
	if req.Query == "" && req.TimeRange == nil {
		return types.SearchRequest{}, errors.New("empty query with no time range")
	}
	return req, nil
}
```

**Pattern:** before reimplementing any search/filter primitive, `grep` for the existing structured query paths (`QueryFlows`, `handleSearch`, `SearchRequest` fields) and wire into them.

**Root cause.** `v0.1.0` was tagged 53 commits behind HEAD and its handlers shipped bodies that violated the OpenAPI contract (`POST /cancel` → `{"status":"ok"}`; `DELETE /sessions/{id}` → `204 No Content`). Since `go get @latest` resolves the highest semver tag from the proxy/VCS tags — not HEAD — consumers kept pulling the stale, non-compliant build. The fix is two-layered: **(1) make the wire bodies compliant at HEAD, (2) re-release a higher tag at HEAD so `@latest` resolves the fixed code.**

### 1. Fix the wire bodies (`api.go`)

Before (shipped in `v0.1.0`):

```go
// BUG: contract says {"cancelled","cancelled_decision_id"}; 404 when unknown
type cancelReply struct{ Status string `json:"status"` }

func (s *server) handleCancel(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request) {
    ...
    writeJSON(w, http.StatusOK, cancelReply{Status: "ok"})   // always "ok"
}

// BUG: contract says 200 {"terminated","session_id"}
func (s *server) handleTerminate(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request) {
    w.WriteHeader(http.StatusNoContent)                       // 204, empty body
}
```

After (the fix, tagged `v0.1.1`):

```go
// CancelResponse: {"cancelled": true, "cancelled_decision_id": "..."}
type CancelResponse struct {
    Cancelled           bool   `json:"cancelled"`
    CancelledDecisionID string `json:"cancelled_decision_id"`
}
type TerminateResponse struct {
    Terminated bool   `json:"terminated"`
    SessionID  string `json:"session_id"`
}

func (s *server) handleCancel(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request) {
    // ...decode req...
    if !s.knownDecision(req.DecisionID) {
        writeJSON(w, http.StatusNotFound, errorResponse{Error: "decision not found"})
        return
    }
    writeJSON(w, http.StatusOK, CancelResponse{Cancelled: true, CancelledDecisionID: req.DecisionID})
}

func (s *server) handleTerminate(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request) {
    id := r.PathValue("id")
    writeJSON(w, http.StatusOK, TerminateResponse{Terminated: true, SessionID: id}) // 200, idempotent
}
```

Regression tests (`api_test.go`) assert: cancel body has `cancelled`+`cancelled_decision_id` and **no** `status` field, cancel-unknown → 404, delete → 200 with `terminated`+`session_id`.

