When a boundary layer accepts an alias/normalization key that a downstream validator checks with `.strict()`, the normalization must **delete the source key as part of the transformation**. Copying alone leaves the object failing strict validation. Recognize the signature: *a 400 whose `unrecognized_keys` list names the very alias documented/accepted at the outer layer.* Always probe the alias live — a dead alias branch is invisible to code reading because both halves look intentional.

